<<<
loan. The verb has been expelled from idiomatic southern English by lend, but was formerly current, & survives in U.S. & locally in U.K.
===
**loan.** The verb has been expelled from idiomatic southern English by *lend,* but was formerly current, & survives in U.S. & locally in U.K.
>>>

<<<
lo(a)th. Loth is recommended. The OED gives precedence to loath, & it is true that that spelling avoids obscuring the connexion with the verb loathe; but in its 19th-c. quotations the proportion is 7 : 1 for loth, a fair proof of establishment.
===
**lo(a)th.** *Loth* is recommended. The OED gives precedence to *loath,* & it is true that that spelling avoids obscuring the connexion with the verb *loathe*; but in its 19th-c. quotations the proportion is 7 : 1 for *loth,* a fair proof of establishment.
>>>

<<<
The verb is always loathe, & loathly & loathsome have always the a.
===
The verb is always *loathe,* & *loathly* & *loathsome* have always the *a.*
>>>

